Matt is a senior with a 4.0 grade point  average I'm sure that's a mistake  his declared major is philosophy and  religion his minor is German he intends  to pursue a future career as either a  pastor or a missionary at Hillsdale he  volunteers for a few good men serves as  a resident advisor in the Simpson  dormitory participates in these students  for life club and belongs to both the  Phi Sigma Tau philosophy honorary in the  Delta Phi Alpha German honorary Matthew  assists with a local Scout troop  and besides his Compact ivities he  recently led a mission trip to the  Ukraine he also belonged to the Boy  Scouts reaching the rank of Eagle Scout  and he worked at the Henson Scout  Reservation Matt is the oldest of three  children his sister Meghan is a college  sophomore his brother Tim is a high  school senior the family lives in Bear  Delaware his father is an information  technology consultant while his mother  is a part-time teacher welcome class of  2018 I'm honored and delighted to speak  to you and to your families today you  don't know me yet although if you live  in Simpson tonight you surely will but  in one sense I already know you  odds are I know what you're thinking and  what you're excited about and even what  you're nervous about that's because  there's three years ago I was sitting  right over there in between my parents  and I was trembling at the thought of  what I got myself into you see I had  heard the rumors of how hard this place  is and it absolutely terrified me and I  suspect that many of you have heard the  rumors too you're probably a tangled  mixture of nerves and anticipation right  now just like I was that's why I would  like to do my best to reassure you and  excite you about the task you are about  to undertake to do that I would like to  start by sharing the story of how I came  to Hillsdale College it begins on a  prospective student day in December 2010  at the Kirby Center in Washington DC at  the end of our tour my dad and I somehow  wound up in the front row to his speech  presented by dr. Arnn in that speech he  told us about Aristotle and explained  Aristotle's concept of nature the idea  that things have  specific ends or purposes to them so for  example he explained that the end of a  horse is to run well and majestically  what he said next though I'll never  forget he paused for a moment and looked  intently at me it's kind of intimidating  and then he looked back at his audience  and he pointed to me he said Aristotle  would look at this boy and he would say  he is not a horse his end is different  it is not simply to run and at that  moment I knew exactly where I had to go  to college  Hillsdale of course but at the same time  you can see the sweet irony of this fact  I chose Hillsdale simply because its  president told me I'm not a horse irony  aside though I've thought about that  moment several times since then as I sat  down to compose this speech I began to  draw more connections between that  moment and the meaning of life at  Hillsdale College you see the fact that  you and I are not horses ought to send a  shock wave right to the core of how we  go about our education we can do more  than work eat and sleep we can do more  than flee pain and pursue pleasure we  can think we can speak we can wonder  imagine and rejoice that is a tremendous  thing a gift and yet it is an almost  forgotten thing in the world today it is  not however a forgotten thing at  Hillsdale College that's why we are here  we long to ask ourselves the deepest  questions to look in the mirror and say  what is man to look at the world and  delight in the beauty of all creation  and ponder the meaning of it all we want  to know what it means to be able to  think to learn to wonder so we come to  this place because it is full of  professors and students who earnestly  pursue the answers to these questions we  come to vote to devote ourselves to this  end together now when I came to  Hillsdale dome I thought I was hot stuff  and I thought I knew what it meant to  take this goal seriously I was one of  those homeschooled kids who worked most  of the time and then spent the rest of  his time worrying about his work as I  thought about it though I've come to  suspect that Hillsdale students in  general tend to fall prey to this idea  that college is simply all about working  hard whether it's in athletics academics  or anywhere and beteen between we've  come to take this seriously  but take it from someone who used to  study long into Friday and Saturday  nights with his dorm room shut tight  that's not what Hillsdale College is all  about  if you treat college like it's only  about working hard building a good  resume tool and a stellar job or even  and here's the tricky one making  yourself the smartest person in the room  then you're actually treating yourself  like a horse good for nothing except  hard grueling work think about our motto  strength rejoices in the challenge  the second word there is key rejoices we  like to look at words like strength and  challenge because these are mighty words  words that seeing of great feats and  accomplishments but they are not the  most important words our motto does not  say strength overcomes the challenge or  strength rises above the challenge even  horses can do these things rather our  motto says strength rejoices in the  challenge during your four years here  you will be totally submerged in the  challenge there is great danger in that  the danger though is not the possibility  that you might - your GPA on the rocks  it's not even that you might drown in a  bottomless sea of tests papers and  reading assignments the danger is that  you could miss the point that you will  excel at all you put your hands to ace  your classes build that glittering  resume become the best athlete on the  field and land a stellar job and yet  settle for a joyless education which is  no education at all but if you let them  the things you study here well as CS  Lewis said prick you with stabs of joy  they will point to something outside  yourself something worth desiring worth  pursuing something you can't just know  about something you have to taste for  yourself and live out but you must see  through your work and look for the  deeper meaning in the books and in the  learning you must live and learn as  though there is something real in this  work and this world before us because  there is and so I hope that you will  soon find a much found of this much  sooner than I ever found that some of  the sweetest moments at Hillsdale  College are the nights when the cell  phones goes silent the laptops are shut  tight and the conversation amongst  friends soars far and wide into the  night it is in those moments that the  notes from the classroom and the  from the books come alive in our life  together and it is then that you will  get a taste of joy of the realness and  meaning of your education in fact it is  only then that your education will truly  begin so as I welcome you to Hillsdale  College I'm really beckoning you to join  us in this fellowship in this  partnership and in this pursuit for joy  I look forward to meeting many of you  and working by your sides and I hope joy  will find you while you are here for the  next four years as you begin though just  do me a favor and remember one thing  you're not a horse
 

